>Of course the old PC,PSW will be overwritten by the new. The old were 
>popped off the stack, and the data is dead. Then new data is pushed on 
>to the stack.

TEST15:
You're correct, and there are three identical results. Isn't it nice.

J11:
SIMH:
E11:
Test15: *rti
Before: R0=100004 SP=740 PC=6612
After:  R0=100004 SP=744 Err=0 SR0=100011 SR1=[0|0] SR2=100004



TEST16:
However, leading on from that starting point I looked at an RTI in
user mode. The user mode stack maps the RTI PC but not the PSW. The
trap is taken in kernel mode of course, so the user mode stack is not
overwritten. In this R0 records the user mode SP.

SimH has a backed-out result and an unaltered user mode stack. The J11
upates SR1 and shows the double pop in the user mode stack. 

SimH:
Test16: *rti  ; [pc,*ps] r0 = user mode stack (unmapped)
Before: R0=77776 SP=744 PC=7174
After:  R0=77776 SP=744 Err=0 SR0=100151 SR1=[0|0] SR2=7174 

J11:
Test16: *rti  ; [pc,*ps] r0 = user mode stack (unmapped)
Before: R0=77776  SP=744 PC=7174
After:  R0=100002 SP=744 Err=0 SR0=100151 SR1=[26|26] SR2=7174 

E11 gets it wrong. The stack is double-popped and, of course, there is
no update information.

E11
Test16: *rti  ; [pc,*ps] r0 = user mode stack (unmapped)
Before: R0=77776 SP=744 PC=7174
After:  R0=100002 SP=744 Err=0 SR0=100151 SR1=[0|0] SR2=7174



TEST17:
This is pretty much the same test as TEST16 using RETURN (RTS PC). The
results are analogous to the previous test:

SimH:
Test17: *return  ; [*pc] r0 = user mode stack (unmapped)
Before: R0=100000 SP=744 PC=7562
After:  R0=100000 SP=744 Err=0 SR0=100151 SR1=[0|0] SR2=7562

J11:
Test17: *return  ; [*pc] r0 = user mode stack (unmapped)
Before: R0=100000 SP=744 PC=7562
After:  R0=100002 SP=744 Err=0 SR0=100151 SR1=[0|26] SR2=7562

E11:
Test17: *rti  ; [*pc] r0 = user mode stack (unmapped)
Before: R0=100000 SP=744 PC=7562 SR0=1
After:  R0=100000 SP=744 Err=0 SR0=100151 SR1=[0|0] SR2=7562

Now, in the earlier tests we've been blithely talking about E11 as
backing-out SR1. But I think what we're seeing is just John's software
realization of the PDP-11 with no particular thought of SR1. In that
context the earlier MFPI problem and the issue in these two tests are
not so much errors as a lack of SR1 implementation.

Ian


