On 2012-02-10, Bob Koehler <koehler@eisner.nospam.encompasserve.org> wrote:
>    IIRC, the issue is that none of the open source UNIX know how
>    to speak MSCP nor TMSCP.  This is a problem only if you use DSSI
>    disks, any other disk will be accessable to UNIX using UNIX
>    filesystems.

I'm not sure I understand what this means.

If the OS has a device driver for MSCP disk devices, it should be able to
talk to any MSCP disk device.  That's the whole point of the MSCP standard,
you get the same interface and the same device driver for all devices of the
same category.

The OS doesn't care if it's a DSSI device, a SCSI device, or an SMD device,
it's all the same from at perspective.

That said, I think 4.2BSD has the uda driver which supports unibus MSCP
controllers, but I don't think it has one for q-buss.  4.3BSD-tahoe definitely
does, though, if you can stand a slightly more bloated kernal.

Or you can use Ultrix-32 which is 4.3 with extra bugs added.
--scott
