>
>     If the Emulex is sitting on the DSSI bus and providing SCSI disks
>     to the DSSI bus, then the storage will look like DSSI disks and
>     BSD won't speak to them.

There are several layers of magic here.

I read through a bunch of replies to this, which also got things wrong, 
but I'm putting the reply here. So some of my comments aren't exactly to 
Bob, but I hope you all get the geist.

MSCP is a protocol to talk to disks. TMSCP is a protocol to talk to 
tapes. The two protocols are extremely similar, but you can't mix them 
on the same communication channel. That is, unlike SCSI, you cannot hang 
both tapes and disks on the same controller.

MSCP have nothing to do with file systems. MSCP is a protocol to talk 
with disks at the block level. MSCP presents a disk as a number of 
consecutive blocks, numbered from 0 and upwards. Easy enough.
So, in theory, any OS that can talk MSCP can talk to any MSCP disk.

Now comes the tricky part: MSCP is a high level protocol, that deals 
with packets. You need something that transport these packets between 
the computer and the disk. That is where DSSI comes in. DSSI is a 
network protocol that can transport packets between different nodes. 
Unless your OS understands DSSI, it cannot talk to the MSCP disks, even 
though both the OS and the disks talk MSCP. Since you don't talk the 
communication channel over which these MSCP packets needs to travel.

Another transport is called SDI.

Yet another transport is CI. (CI actually terminates in another 
controller, from which you normally then have SDI.)

Any Unix-like OS apart from Ultrix actually only knows how to talk with 
directly attached controllers like the UDA-50. The UDA-50 have four SDI 
ports, but what the programming interface might be called I don't know. 
But it's a rather simple packet interface anyway.

So now we are down to controller, which are programmed in different ways.

There are CI controller, DSSI controllers, and UDA-50 controllers, which 
implements different packet transports.

Controllers that implement the UDA-50 interface are:
UDA-50
KDA-50
RQDXn
RQZX1

Also, all SCSI controllers from CMD, Viking, Emulex and others, which 
implements MSCP are actually also implementing the same packet interface 
as the UDA-50.

So, if you have an OS that can talk with any of these controllers, it 
can talk with all of them.

Now, what sits at the far end of the controller in turn, can be MFM 
(RQDXn controllers), DSI (RA-disks using UDA-50 et.al), SCSI (RQZX1, 
various 3rd party controllers), DSSI disk controllers, or CI disk 
controllers. But that don't matter. As soon as you both have an MSCP 
device driver in your OS, which the OS can use to access the disk, and a 
port device driver, which the MSCP device driver use to actually talk 
across the transport, you are all set.

The MSCP driver alone will not do it. Which is why, even though NetBSD 
have a MSCP device driver, cannot talk with DSSI disks, nor CI disks. 
There are no port drivers for those transports.

	Johnny


