It is remarkable that IBM and DEC both had "thinkers" in key positions
at the time. Thomas Brooks' MYTHICAL MAN MONTH evolves the (radical
for IBM then) ideas that user friendliness and small teams were the
way to go. That's what DEC were doing. Gordon Bell's two key books
include a study of IBM machines, including the 360. Both of Bell's
books are on-line.

http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/people/gbell/Computer_Engineering/index.html
http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/people/gbell/Computer_Structures__Readings_and_Examples/index.html

>> Now, the problem for the PDP-11 was that it remained sensitive to
>> technology changes and frequently had to modify the architecture
>> because of the available technology. To use the earlier analogy of the
>> piano, sheet music played on the various PDP-11s would sound different
>> without changes to the sheet music.
>
>It does seem like IBM did better at keeping its architecture
>consistent through the generations. Maybe partly luck, and
>partly that things were more clearly defined in the first place.

IBM had "process" completely under control. Brooks could hire a 1000
programmers and the IBM internal systems just took over. DEC was run
by cowboys pioneering in the wild west. Gordon Bell rarely knew what
his engineers were really doing.

I used to be heavily involved in DECUS Europe and I pushed for Bell to
come over and give a keynote address. He was quite amazing and
presented a picture of himself ensconced in his office along with his
mania for making charts of everything. He never got what he wanted
from his engineers. He loved what they did, but it wasn't what he
needed. So he made this beautiful chart where he mapped what he knew
were all the favorite habits of each of his designers, and then, to
get the products he wanted, he simply selected the right group of
"habits" to form teams. He was a lot of fun.

In other papers he describes an emotionally-charged decision making
process. Bob Supnik's on-line papers paint the same picture. There was
one case where they made an official decision to build a particular
system but simply never got around to doing it. This is "seat of your
pants" stuff.

In retrospect, the PDP-11 was the learning curve they needed for the
VAX. If you go back and look at the early PDP-11 documentation you see
a company that has yet to invent itself.

