>
>It didn't last quite as long as they may have hoped, but
>not so bad, either.

But VMS is alive and well, living in "bliss"... DEC thought of
themselves too much as a hardware company, but I think their lasting
innovations were in software. I have the VMS source fiches for V3x,and
maybe V4. They're quite magical--to see this small group of software
engineers ripping out page after page of excellent code. Volumes of
it. VMS isn't my favorite working environment, but the code is a
monument to an understanding of what an O/S is. 

CPU architectures have lost their central role. Today it's the
"software architectures", and principally based on the two streams
which emanated from the PDP-11 and VAX: the VMS/Windows stream and the
Unix stream.

