contains code which manages all the architectural variations. RT-11
won't boot on a T11/SBC-11 unless you have the version of RT-11 which
inserts NOPs here and there to manage the interrupt scheme. 

Code developed exclusively on an LSI-11 often crashs when moved to
other PDP-11s because the LSI-11 does not implement odd address traps.
That has always been a major drag.

PDP-11 software was frequently distributed in a variety of
NHD/EIS/FPU/FIS/EAE versions.

I have noted in earlier posts that all PDP-11s share the same "base
architecture", and that's where most of the compatibility comes from.
That's not what the disagreement is about. What you call
"implementation" variations are what I generally call "(model)
architectural variations". 

