>
>The solution would actually be to make sure the address in userspace you 
>want to read/write is in memory, so that you don't get page faults. 
>Using memory mapping registers for this wouldn't be fun.
>One more trick that can be used is to only support/use MFPx/MTPx in 
>kernel in the sequence of a push to the stack followed by this. That 
>way, you could, as a part of the restart detect this special case, and 
>back up to the previous instruction to redo the push as well.

My thoughts too. The problem is that the "previous instruction" could
be 2, 4 or 6 bytes long. Noting that MPTI always clears the V-bit, I
would follow the MPTI instruction with BVS, as below, and use the BVS
opcode itself as a signature for the special behavior. I seem to
recall using this technique to solve   

10$:	psh	<value>
	mtpi	<address>
	bvs	10$

(In fact I already use this kind of thing in RUST/XM to handle bus
errors (trap to 4) instead of address checking in some cases.)

"Operational" solutions are available to the other issues as well. For
example, MFPS will never fail to a general register. 


Ian


