>> for example create a file, or use the command line parser.
>
> I don't think your previous "discussion" was with me, or least I hope
> it wasn't, because the view you present is completely inaccurate.

I don't remember who it was with. Not important anyway.

> The "USR" is to RT-11 what the "F11ACP" is to RSX. You can't describe
> the scheduling dynamics of the RSX kernel on the basis of the F11ACP
> behavior and you can't describe RT-11's scheduling dynamics on the
> basis of the USR behavior. They're quite different things.

F11ACP is a process, scheduled just like any other process. And you are 
allowed to have as many of them as you want.

> The RT-11 USR is a sub-system which handles ACP-like services, driver
> fetch and the like. Access to the USR (ACP) is queued on a system-wide
> basis. I am not sure whether the RSX F11ACP is single-threaded for all
> devices or not.

It is hard to describe F11ACP in that way. First of all, you are allowed 
to have several F11ACP running, one for each device if you want to. 
Second, it is not really single-threaded as such. It is threading on a 
device level, though. You cannot have several opens or creates going on 
in parallel on a single device. The first file needs to be created 
before the second one can, for example (almost an obvious comment, but 
anyway...)
But you can have several creates being done on different devices at the 
same time.
The reason for wanting to have a separate F11ACP for your device is 
caching. F11ACP cache file headers, but there is only so much caching 
that can be done in one process. Having a separate process allows it to 
have a separate cache, since this cache is held in the process memory.

> The RT-11 CSI (Command String Interpreter) was originally located in
> the USR. These days, in multi-tasking RT-11, initial CSI processing is
> handled outside the USR. Programs don't have to queue for command
> processing.

Looking at the source, it would appear that anyone calling anything 
related to USR will lock everything else out from calling USR. I can see 
CSI in there, but it might be that some additional trickery is located 
somewhere.

But it's way more locking things out than RSX ever does. Not to mention 
that a process doing a call to open or create a file actually is not 
blocked in RSX. It can continue running happily on for ever before that 
call completes.

>> But to be clear, RSX protects the whole kernel through a lock, and does
>> not depend on system state or processor priority levels (apart from the
>> actual lock operations, which are using the IPL itself to protect them).
>> Which is why adding MP to RSX was also a rather simple operation.
>
> By "system state" I mean basically the same state as "fork state". In
> RSX that also involves a switch to the "system stack". System state
> runs at IPL 0. $DIRSV switches to system state.

Ah. $DIRSV in RSX is the way you get to "system state" from a user mode 
system call. You can also get there through an interrupt, which then 
gets through $INTSI.
In reality, the "system state" is visible as the stack depth value. When 
it is 0, you are in system state. If it's +1 you are in user mode, and 
if it is <0, you are in an interrupt handler. Anything <1 means you are 
in kernel mode.
(You can also get there through $INTSC, $INTSE and $INTSV)

>> RT-11 plays with the IPL at various places to protect code which is
>> sensitive.
