The opening section of code is more or less duplicated in RMONFB.MAC
(later RMON.MAC). Look for the "C$SIGN:". The code in the USR is only
used for the single-threaded single-user RT-11/SJ (and later for
RT-11/SB). 

>But it's way more locking things out than RSX ever does.

That's confusing the "ACP" with the system. 

> Not to mention 
>that a process doing a call to open or create a file actually is not 
>blocked in RSX. It can continue running happily on for ever before that 
>call completes.

I don't recall anyone *ever* requesting an asynchronous USR under
RT-11. It's just not functionality which is needed most of the time.
When I have needed it, there have been workarounds. In one instance I
was able to keep the continuous activity going in chained ASTs. In
another case I used two tasks. That was a long time ago.

The RT-11 approach to ACP operations  is more like VMS or Windows.
The need to open files asynchronously occurs fairly rarely. All
systems have their little advantages here and there. In RUST/XM each
process effectively has it's own private generic ACP. Multiple
processes can perform overlapping file opens on the same volume.
Processes only queue when they want to modify the directory structure
of the same volume. Now, that's probably something you can't do on
RSX, but I wouldn't use the difference to characterize RSX as doing
"way more locking than" RUST/XM does. It's a nice feature. I seem to
recall that later versions of VMS did something similar.

