>Yes. Exactly. And all of that kind of operations are in RSX done at the 
>fork level, without IPL protection, because it is not needed.

It could also be handled in RT-11 by entering and then leaving system
state. But, practically speaking, there is no advantage in entering
system state to protect a mere three instructions since even more
instructions execute at IPL 7 by entering and leaving system state.

>That's what the forks are for. If RT-11 don't use forks for this, what 
>do RT-11 use forks for?

Fork is used by drivers. ENSYS performs the same functionality for the
monitor code. There are about twenty tasks performed by ENSYS in the
RT-11 monitor. A later section of the MARKTM code uses ENSYS to switch
to system state to insert the mark time request in the queue. The
other system calls involving the timer queue switch to system state at
the appropriate time. I/O code switches to system state to insert I/O
elements into I/O queues and to call drivers. Extended memory is
allocated and deallocated in system state. 

But on the matter of IPL and RSX, I note that RSX also raises IPL to
complete certain tasks, in the system and in drivers. Indeed, I count
about 5000! occurrences of raised IPL in the RSX sources (by searching
for ";;;").

>> However, the  primary tool that RT-11 uses for system (scheduler)
>> synchronisation is ENSYS, which switches to system state (fork state).
>> There are about twenty calls to $ENSYS in the resident monitor.
>
>Yes, I can see a bunch of calls to ENSYS. And as far as I can tell, 
>ENSYS raises the SPL to 7, before coming back to where it was invoked. 
>Locking out all interrupts every time. Each routine can then, when it 
>suitable, drop the IPL. 

Incorrect. ENSYS raises IPL for exactly four instructions (in the
mapped monitor) and then lowers it back to zero. The mask in the final
instruction (BIC (R4)+,@R4) is always "PR7" for ENSYS. ENSYS code do
not choose when to drop IPL, as it has already been returned to zero.
IPL is not used for the purposes you indicate.

	SPL	7	;Enter here to switch into System State
$INTEN::MOV	R4,-(SP)	;;; Save R4
	INC	(PC)+		;;; Bump level pointer
INTLVL:: .WORD	-1		;;;  Interrupt level
	MOV	#<PS>,R4	;;; R4 -> PS
	BIC	(R5)+,@R4	;;; Down to handler priority

(In fact, if you examine the routine above, the raised IPL is
completely redundant. ENSYS raises IPL to imitate the environment of a
driver ISR. $INTEN is the code which lowers priority for a driver. In
the case of a driver the (R5)+ mask can be used to lower IPL to device
IPL).

>There is also this FRK routine, which I assumed 
>was how you created a fork in RT-11, but I might have missed something. 
>The only thing calling FRK is SP.MAC, that I can see.

Drivers use the driver macro .FORK to fork. It usually comes within a
few instructions of .DRAST, which is the driver ISR entry point.

>> The idea that RT-11 manages system synchronisation via IPL is simply
>> wrong. System state and queues are used to manage synchronisation.
>
>I obviously find it different when looking. ENSYS uses the IPL (always 
>raising it to 7). Various functions like the mark time uses the IPL to 
>protect structures. I didn't say "synchronization", but "protection". 
>You can find it in lots of places, like I said.

ENSYS is exactly the same as DIRSV in this regard. So, if you want to
argue that ENSYS's brief use of IPL means that ENSYS is using IPL to
protect structures then you have to accept that DIRSV's brief use of
IPL implies that DIRSV is also using IPL to protect structures. I
think that's silly. IPL is being used for a short act of hardware
protection so that a process may enter system state. It is then system
state which provides the protection, not IPL.

Indeed, I quote from an earlier statement by yourself above:

  The fork routine does not run with raised IPL. It's only the 
  enquing/dequeing of the fork block that is protected by the IPL,
  just like I said.

Likewise, ENSYS code does not run with raised IPL. It's only the entry
and exit code which is protected by IPL--the exit code has to check
for pending fork routines and the like.

