Are you saying that VMS system services cannot themselves issue system
service calls? That was my point.

Regarding DCL, RMS etc, I think a case could be made for considering
RMS operations such as $OPEN, $CLOSE etc for being system calls under
VMS since they are the typical method by which files are opened and
closed. This functionality is certainly considered to be system
functionality under RT-11 and Windows. However, the point is not
necessary for my argument.

>> The architecture of the RUST/XM kernel was based on a mixture of VMS
>> and RT-11. There were few problems harmonising the two systems because
>> they are fundamentally similar. Indeed, the system dynamics of RT-11,
>> RUST/XM, RSX and VMS are constructed using the same building blocks.
>> The principal difference is that RSX puts a system state wrapper
>> around its system calls where the other systems do not.
>
>VMS is very closely engineered like RSX in so many ways that I would 
>find it odd if you could substantiate that it works more like RT-11. But 
>I have been surprised before, and am ready for it again.

I'm not saying that in general terms VMS is *more* like RSX than
RT-11. That's simply not the case. The roots of VMS are found in RSX.
I'm pointing to a particular set of behaviors where VMS dropped some
the RSX behavior and did things in a way which was more like RT-11.
I'm not saying that they were influenced by RT-11 either. I think they
did what anyone does when they write a new system: keep the good bits
and get rid of the weak points of the previous system.

Ian


