It is somewhat popular when you want to maximize performance in RSX. 
Keep doing other work while file operations are being processed. 
Asynchronous I/O, you know...

But when it don't exist, I would not expect people to ask for it.

> The RT-11 approach to ACP operations  is more like VMS or Windows.

Nonsense. VMS works exactly the same way as RSX in this aspect. You have 
one or more ACPs in VMS, just like RSX. And you queue I/O operations to 
it, and your program can continue doing whatever while the I/O operation 
is being completed. This I *know*.

In more recent versions of VMS, they moved much of the ACP code into the 
kernel, to improve performance by avoiding the context switching, so you 
have something called an XQP doing the ACP work, but the interface did 
not change.
And you still have the ACP around, for some operations.
You'll see it if you look at what processes are running.

I can possibly buy that RT-11 works like windows, but mostly because I 
don't know how windows works, so I can buy any claims for it. :-)

> The need to open files asynchronously occurs fairly rarely. All
> systems have their little advantages here and there. In RUST/XM each
