RT-11 is (in my eyes) to convoluted and tricky to follow. You have to 
read the code many times, and go back and forth, and trying to remember 
exactly what state you are in at every point, to understand what it is 
doing.

Hmm, if I read things right, RT-11 work the same way as Unix, in that 
you are running with the user context, while in the kernel, part of the 
time.
So, sometimes you protect shared data through raising the IPL, sometimes 
through mutexes, and sometimes through going through ENSYS. ENSYS will 
protect your code from interrupt handles which create forks to do work. 
IPL (obviously) protects you from even getting interrupts (and thus also 
protects you against interrupt handlers), while mutexes basically 
protect you against other processes.

But I'm not trying to argue that SPL would be more expensive than 
creating a fork. But I am arguing that having your priority changed all 
over the place makes it harder if you write code to make sure it is 
properly protected, since you need to know where and how you come into 
that piece of code so you can protect it right, and also not 
accidentally drop the IPL at the wrong place. It also makes is much 
harder if you ever want to move to a system where the IPL level is not 
enough (such as in MP), as you have to identify whereever you are 
raising the IPL, and get something like spinning locks in place at all 
those places.

