> do, but it is not possible in either VMS or RSX.
> When you open a file in those operating systems, you can specify if
> others are allowed to open the file or not, and thus you must have a
> clear concept of one process opening the file completely before another
> can, so you can enforce protections like that.
> So it is doing more locking that RUST/XM does. And it provides more
> services, by doing this.

(And obviously at a cost...)

>>>> RT-11 uses IPL for hardware synchonisation in just the same way that
>>>> RSX does. The fork routine is a good example. Here's the RSX code.

A clarification of my view might help here:
Both OSes use IPL to protect/synchronize access to some structures. 
However, in RSX this is basically just one structure, while RT-11 uses 
the IPL way to protect many things.

In RSX, protection from multiple processes and drivers manipulating 
shared data in an uncontrolled way is through the fork. No other way is 
allowed. You cannot bump just bump the IPL and then think you are safe 
in RSX. So it is not done. This is different than RT-11, where this is 
clearly done. The RSX way is not cheaper, but it does make things more 
uniform and easy to follow (I think), and it makes it much easier to 
make the system work in an MP environment.

I'm not sure I'd use the term "hardware synchronization", since it is 
essentially synchronization between processes. Yes, IPLs are also used 
for hardware synchronization, but that is essentially talking about 
interrupt handlers preventing additional interrupts while processing one 
(or at least that is my quick reflection on what hardware 
synchronization is).


>> But on the matter of IPL and RSX, I note that RSX also raises IPL to
>> complete certain tasks, in the system and in drivers. Indeed, I count
>> about 5000! occurrences of raised IPL in the RSX sources (by searching
>> for ";;;").
>
> Yes. Not surprised, since all instructions in device drivers which
> happens between an interrupt, and the creation of the fork, for example,
> will be running at a raised IPL.
> Also, if you search for ";;;", you also be getting a lot of empty lines,
> which is just because comments are running over several lines, and each
> line is prefixed with ;;;, if the code being commented is running at a
> raised IPL. But it's arguable how relevant empty lines with just a
> comment is...
>
> Another nice point here is that device drivers structures are not being
> manipulated by more than one CPU, since a device can only be attached to
> one CPU at a time, so those pieces are the only ones where IPL is
> enough, and you don't have to worry about MP issues in RSX. The fork
> queue, however, is shared between all processors, and needs to be
> properly protected.

