If you have a single disk, then yes, calls like open and close will be 
serialized for that disk. But you can still do operations on other 
disks. A single F11ACP does not serialize access to different disks, so 
the point is the number of disks, not the number of ACPs.

> The RSX systems I use have a single F11ACP, and since they mostly have
> a single system device there wouldn't be much point in having more
> than one. I don't think any of the software I use depends on
> asynchronous directory services. It's just not something most folk
> need to have. In previous posts I've described how one can circumvent
> the USR queueing behavior when it's actually necessary.

Indeed, if you only have one disk, it is totally pointless to have more 
than one ACP, as a device can only be using one ACP.
But a single ACP does not mean that the ACP is single threaded. It 
performs operations on different disks in parallel.

>>>>>> RT-11 uses IPL for hardware synchonisation in just the same way that
