> possible by selectively using a range of devices. RSX wraps everything
> in system state. It gains some simplicity in doing so but loses, I
> think, by increasing scheduling and AST latencies.

I'll happily challenge that. Latencies in RSX are very good in fact. If 
anything, VMS could do with some improvement (rip out the cruft added, 
and get back to its roots).

The claim that RT-11 avoids blocking the scheduler is true in as much as 
scheduling can happen sometimes while the process is inside the kernel. 
However, RT-11 blocks processes from some operations if any other 
process is doing the same (or similar operation), which makes the 
avoided scheduling block more like smoke than actual value.

And interrupts are occasionally locked out in various places in RT-11, 
even if for just short intervals, while they are locked out much less in 
RSX.

> The RSX approach of wrapping system calls in system state means that
> system calls can't afford to hang about in the kernel because of the
> increased scheduling and AST latencies. That's a hard discipline to
> maintain across the life of a system. System creep is unavoidable. I
> don't have access to the M+ sources, but I notice a significant
> increase in the number of executive directives and think to myself: "I
> bet some of these are taking a lot longer than would have been
> permitted in early versions of RSX."

Right. System calls cannot afford to hang around. They are always 
required to finish without any sleeps, delays, mutex hangs, or so on.
It is really not a hard discipline to follow, I think. In fact, I think 
it is wonderful, makes a lot of sense, and is much better than the 
alternative. However, I do recognize that it takes a little more 
thinking, and a different approach to some problems than the 
traditional, straight forward one. But it is an effort well worth it.
And no, the "new" system services in M+ do not take much more time than 
the "old" ones. It still behaves the same way. If it would not, then the 
system would not be usable, and RSX systems have been used 
(traditionally) in plenty of hard realtime system applications. That's 
the normal place where you still find RSX systems (yes, I know of plenty 
still in use in the industry).
It's partly just because of the very good response times and latencies 
that RSX was so successful.

