>10$:    EMT     374            ; .purge #8., .purge #7, etc
>         DECB    R0            ; next channel
>         BGE     10$           ; more channels to do
>         BR      11$           ; join common code

EMT 374, subcode 3 is .PURGE. Channels 8. down to 0 are purged. .PURGE
checks the conditions I listed.

>If I remember right, this is from RT-11 V5.5, so it might be that things 
>got changed after that?

It's pretty much the same.


<snip>
>> I could extend the functionality to handle access control fairly
>> easily. It would just be a matter of having a queue for open
>> operations with potential sharing conflicts.
>
>Right. But then you need to figure out if an open request is for a file 
>that is already open or not, and then do two different paths, depending 
>on that. And the paths are pretty much the same, except without the 
>shared access checks on one of them. And if you have lots of open files, 
>then the checking can become a noticeable part of the operation. But 
>yes, there are possibly some optimization that could be done here. I 
>have not looked deep enough at it to tell for sure if they would be 
>enough to be worth the code.

At some point in the OPEN process you'd have to "register" an open
with the fully qualified, normalized file name/id and the access mode.


<snip>
>> But that's a bad thing because it's put everything into fork/system
>> and that increases AST and scheduling latencies. RT-11 and VMS can
>> perform most of their system call tasks with no impact at all on AST
>> delivery or scheduling latencies. That's the real issue we're talking
>> about.
>
>Instead potentially affecting interrupt latencies... :-)

Latency is typically measured as "average" and "worst" case.
Occasional short sequences of four or so instructions doesn't alter
either of those measures.

>As you are not expected to spend large amounts of time in forks in RSX, 
>this is not really a big issue. But yes, forks do affect AST delivery 
>and task execution (let's take the big picture here, scheduling is just 
>a subset of task execution, which is really what is being delayed by forks).
>But since you only have one CPU, it can either be running a user 
>process, or run the fork. The fork needs to be completed sooner or 
>later, especially since the device related to the fork cannot issue 
>another fork until the previous is completed. Is there a point in 
>delaying the fork?

The point is that in RSX an executive directive is effectively a "fork
routine", and these directives can take quite a considerable time to
complete. Creating a new task is not a simple, short process. The
worst-case fork latency in RSX is the time it takes to execute the
longest executive directive.

It is utterly clear that VMS could not handled system calls in system
state--thousands of instructions are sometimes required.

