On 2012-02-21 07.17, paramucho wrote:
> On Mon, 20 Feb 2012 11:28:14 +0100, Johnny Billquist<bqt@softjar.se>
> wrote:
>
>> On 2012-02-20 06.53, paramucho wrote:
>>> On Sat, 18 Feb 2012 21:58:28 +0100, Johnny Billquist<bqt@softjar.se>
>>> wrote:
>>>
>>>> On 2012-02-18 12:55, paramucho wrote:
>>>>> On Fri, 17 Feb 2012 18:13:44 +0100, Johnny Billquist<bqt@softjar.se>
>>>>> wrote:
>>>>>
>>> The point is that in RSX an executive directive is effectively a "fork
>>> routine", and these directives can take quite a considerable time to
>>> complete. Creating a new task is not a simple, short process. The
>>> worst-case fork latency in RSX is the time it takes to execute the
>>> longest executive directive.
>>
>> Right in that all system calls are forks. Crating a new task in RSX is
>> not really done as a system call. It's done by a task. The only system
>> call is to invoke an existing task.
>> Once again, RSX do not sit in system calls for extended times. Things
>> that take long times are done in processes, not the system. And can thus
>> also be done in parallel by several users.
>
> But if RSX didn't go into system state you wouldn't need these helper
> processes. At present they're not an option.

But then you would have to be able to block in the system call, at which 
point you also need to be able to back out and restart system calls, or 
else fail system calls with the error saying that you need to redo the 
system call (EINTR). One is extremely complex, while the other is very 
ugly. Unix and VMS have gone with the latter.
I thought we'd already agreed that Unix was not something to follow. :-)

Or we could accept that processes become deaf at certain times, making 
response times utterly unpredictable.

>>> It is utterly clear that VMS could not handled system calls in system
>>> state--thousands of instructions are sometimes required.
>>
>> It is also clear that response times is VMS are much worse than RSX.
>> Easily demonstrated by having an RSX and a VMS system on the same
>> ethernet allowing DECservers to be booted. They will be booted by the
>> first system that responds, which is the RSX system every time, even
>> when you have much faster VAX hardware.
>
> That's a boot test, not a latency test.

No. I'm not talking about how long it takes for the DECserver to boot. 
I'm talking about how fast you respond to the boot request.
The DECserver broadcasts a boot request on the ethernet. Other machines 
listen for that, and respond to requests saying that they are willing to 
provide the boot service. The first such response that comes back to the 
DECserver is used by the DECserver for the following boot. The RSX 
systems on the network responds much faster to the boot *requests* than 
the VMS systems.

>> And VMS actually have the same horrible possibilities that Unix do. You
>> can have system calls that block, and return with errors saying you
>> should retry the system call again (I just remembered having a
>> discussion about VMS mailboxes, which ended up with this conclusion a
>> few months ago).
>
> You're looking at that behavior through RSX eyes. VMS system calls
> begin as an extension of user code in user state. Nothing is blocked.

Right. And system calls in VMS can later on be blocked, and even fail 
with a return code saying you should just retry the system call again 
because it failed the last time for some random reason that will not be 
the same on the next try.

> It's a different system philosphy, and clearly a decision they made in
> terms of changing their approach from that of RSX.

Indeed. Lots of the extensions that VMS added to the RSX base are more 
wart-like. The clean bottom line is still there, but some of the 
additions have a different philosophy.

>>> I must be getting old...I'm starting to forget RT-11...
>>
>> I occasionally get the same feeling about RSX when talking with you. I
>> really need to go and check stuff that I normally don't even think
>> about. :-)
>
> Did you get the two e-mails I sent, or are they landing in a
> bit-bucket?

Sorry, yes. I got them. Have not had time to do much. Last week was 
ridiculous at work...

	Johnny


