>> There are other still "undocumented" features in RT-11 such
>> as READ  ONLY / WRITE  PROTECTED files.
>
> They are directory entry flags which were defined in the final stages
> of RT-11. They were barely used. The Read-only flag is copied by
> .LOOKUP, but that's about it. I haven't bothered implementing it
> because the new flags are not backwards compatible with earlier
> versions of RT-11 (which is, historically, partly my fault).

Am I understanding correct that the "P" after the file size is the 
indication of the read-only status of the file? I seem to remember it 
appearing to be so from something I did, but I'm not sure.

>>> RT-11 magtape and cassette drivers are "special devices" with their
>>> own built-in ACPs. Directory operations on these devices may be
>>> handled via the USR or by using .SPFUN (special functions) to
>>> communicate directly with the driver. In this case the operation is
>>> analogous to the RSX QIO interface. Since tape devices can be
>>> particularly time consuming it is sometimes advantageous to handle
>>> their directory operations without blocking the USR.
>>>
>>>
>> I can't remember if the USR is unblocked when a "special
>> device" requests the USR to hand off what is normally a
>> request handled by the USR.  I seem to remember that the
>> USR is invoked initially when a file is opened on a magtape,
>> but I can't remember what happens when the device driver
>> takes over to handle the open request.  Do you know if
>> the USR in RT-11 is unblocked at that point?  Often,
>> the program may not even know that the device for the
>> file open request is a "special device" and simply call the
>> USR with the usual EMT request for the type of open
>> (.LookUp,  .Enter) which is required.
>
> The USR waits for any .LOOKUP/.ENTER to complete.

So, if I understand correctly, any program that don't understand that an 
access to a magtape can be done with .SPFUN, and instead opens it with 
USR will block until the magtape file have been opened, which can take a 
serious while?

Can a program that is waiting on USR be interrupted by something 
AST-like? What happens to the USR call meanwhile?

	Johnny


