>> How does the process become deaf? It can still accept ASTs. What else
>> is it supposed to do?
>
>It wasn't a statement - it was a suggestive question on what to possibly do.
>The problem is - if you get an AST while blocking on a system call, what 
>will you do with that system call. The program can do another system 
>call in the AST. Can you deal with being blocked in a system call, and 
>at the same time do another system call? And what do you expect the 
>stack to look like at that point? And the blocking in the current system 
>call is somehow reflected in the process status. That needs to be 
>temporary "forgotten", since the process is not blocked while running in 
>the AST. But once the AST is finished, I would assume that you expect 
>the process to go back to being blocked in the outer system call. But 
>the process state might have changed because of things done in the AST, 
>including the wish to get out of the system call you were blocked in. 
>Not to mention what to do if you are blocking on a system call, gets an 
>AST, and in the AST handler do a system call which blocks you. Then what?

System waits ("blocks") may terminate prematurely because of various
conditions, including stuff that ASTs do. So, when a system wait
returns, the routine has to make sure that it's actual condition has
been satisfied, and if it hasn't then it reissues the system wait. The
stack is not a problem.

In the RT-11 sources you'll see a bunch of calls to $SYSWT which
implement this logic for RT-11. $SYSWT uses co-routines, so they're
even more convoluted than RT-11 usually is.

I had a look at my RUST/XM system wait code tonight, and I think I
understand it. The "$stall #10$, prwhi" issues the system wait, with
10$ as the continuation address after the system wait and prwhi.
(hibernation) as the system wait state.

em$spd::				;suspend
10$:	mov	p$hpcr,r4		;get the pcr 
	$float				;stop the scheduler
	mov	pr.sta(r4),r0		;get the status
	bic	#prwak$,pr.sta(r4) 	;clear the bit
	bit	#stact$,p$hast		;are we in an AST?
	bne	20$			;yes - forget the rest
	bit	#prwak$,r0		;did we get a wake up?
	bne	20$			;yes
	$stall	#10$, prwhi.		;hibernate
20$:	$land
	return

em$rsm::				;resume
	mov	p$hpcr,r4		;get the pcr
	bis	#prwak$,pr.sta(r4) 	;set wake pending
	return

>See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PCLSRing for more details.

That seems to be some other issue.

