On 2012-02-21 17.42, paramucho wrote:
> On Tue, 21 Feb 2012 15:16:25 +0100, Johnny Billquist<bqt@softjar.se>
> wrote:
>
>> On 2012-02-21 12.37, paramucho wrote:
>>> On Tue, 21 Feb 2012 11:23:02 +0100, Johnny Billquist<bqt@softjar.se>
>>> wrote:
>>>
>>> <snip?>
>>>
>>>>> But if RSX didn't go into system state you wouldn't need these helper
>>>>> processes. At present they're not an option.
>>>>
>>>> But then you would have to be able to block in the system call, at which
>>>> point you also need to be able to back out and restart system calls, or
>>>> else fail system calls with the error saying that you need to redo the
>>>> system call (EINTR). One is extremely complex, while the other is very
>>>> ugly. Unix and VMS have gone with the latter.
>>>> I thought we'd already agreed that Unix was not something to follow. :-)
>>>>
>>>> Or we could accept that processes become deaf at certain times, making
>>>> response times utterly unpredictable.
>>>
>>> How does the process become deaf? It can still accept ASTs. What else
>>> is it supposed to do?
>>
>> It wasn't a statement - it was a suggestive question on what to possibly do.
>> The problem is - if you get an AST while blocking on a system call, what
>> will you do with that system call. The program can do another system
>> call in the AST. Can you deal with being blocked in a system call, and
>> at the same time do another system call? And what do you expect the
>> stack to look like at that point? And the blocking in the current system
>> call is somehow reflected in the process status. That needs to be
>> temporary "forgotten", since the process is not blocked while running in
>> the AST. But once the AST is finished, I would assume that you expect
>> the process to go back to being blocked in the outer system call. But
>> the process state might have changed because of things done in the AST,
>> including the wish to get out of the system call you were blocked in.
>> Not to mention what to do if you are blocking on a system call, gets an
>> AST, and in the AST handler do a system call which blocks you. Then what?
>
> System waits ("blocks") may terminate prematurely because of various
> conditions, including stuff that ASTs do. So, when a system wait
> returns, the routine has to make sure that it's actual condition has
> been satisfied, and if it hasn't then it reissues the system wait. The
> stack is not a problem.

So you need to restart the system call. What if it is a system call that 
have been partially completed? The wait does not happen as a unique part 
of the call.

Oh, wait, I bet you are thinking in terms of RT-11 here, where the 
distinction between user mode and kernel mode is rather blurry.

In Unix, as an example, there is no context around to restart in the 
middle of a system call. What you have left is the context at user mode, 
which is then the start of the system call again.
And this is because a signal is delivered in user mode, so the process 
gets out of the kernel, in order to run the signal handler. There is no 
way you can keep the kernel context for the user process around at that 
point. It can not live on the kernel stack, since you exited from the 
kernel. It can not live on the user stack, since that would allow you to 
break the security of the kernel.

> In the RT-11 sources you'll see a bunch of calls to $SYSWT which
> implement this logic for RT-11. $SYSWT uses co-routines, so they're
> even more convoluted than RT-11 usually is.
>
> I had a look at my RUST/XM system wait code tonight, and I think I
> understand it. The "$stall #10$, prwhi" issues the system wait, with
> 10$ as the continuation address after the system wait and prwhi.
> (hibernation) as the system wait state.
>
> em$spd::				;suspend
> 10$:	mov	p$hpcr,r4		;get the pcr
> 	$float				;stop the scheduler
> 	mov	pr.sta(r4),r0		;get the status
> 	bic	#prwak$,pr.sta(r4) 	;clear the bit
> 	bit	#stact$,p$hast		;are we in an AST?
> 	bne	20$			;yes - forget the rest
> 	bit	#prwak$,r0		;did we get a wake up?
> 	bne	20$			;yes
> 	$stall	#10$, prwhi.		;hibernate
> 20$:	$land
> 	return
>
> em$rsm::				;resume
> 	mov	p$hpcr,r4		;get the pcr
> 	bis	#prwak$,pr.sta(r4) 	;set wake pending
> 	return
>
>> See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PCLSRing for more details.
>
> That seems to be some other issue.

No. It is totally the issue. See my comments above.

RT-11 can, however, get away with some things that you cannot do in 
systems where user mode and kernel mode are totally separate and 
isolated from each other.

Assuming I'm right, I can see why a lot of discussion and confusion 
comes up between us. The conditions in RT-11 are rather different than 
other systems, meaning you can do stuff that is totally impossible in 
other OSes.

>>> It's still just one specific test. That doesn't measure overall system
>>> performance or other system specific behaviors.
>>
>> Sure. I can dig up a number of more examples showing that response times
>> in VMS are much worse than RSX. How many do you need to be convinced?
>
> How long is a piece of string :-)

Exactly. :-D

> I once purchased a VAX/730, so you don't need to convince me of
> anything...

Heh. That was one slow machine.

> But I also spent about five years or so working with VMS and it ain't
> so bad and I learned a lot by reading the VMS source microfiches.

VMS have its points... But to quote the RSX sources:
(LB:[11,10]TDSCH.MAC, line 130 or so...)

;+
;  If we cannot process a clock interrupt within 10 seconds, we are
