>Are you suggesting that the PROTECTED bit in the
>status word of a file label ever changed from the highest
>order bit (bit 15 = 100000 making a word negative) over
>the course of RT-11?  Bit 14 (040000) is used as the
>READ  ONLY bit and is always compatible with the
>other bits that are used.

If you in V5.4 USR.MAC you'll find the following check made in
BLKCHK:

BLKCHK: ...
	BIT	#^C<107400>,@R1
	BNE	ILLDIR

That code checks for an invalid volume by making sure the bits 
070377 are not set in the directory entry status word. My part,
historically, was that I got the RT-11 team to put that check in
because it was the only cause I had of crashes in STAR-11, my earliest
commercial system. 

The way I got them to put the check in was this: The size of the USR
was fixed at exactly 2048 words. Anytime they wanted to put more code
in there they'd have to spend days just optimising to get a few free
words. I had a look at the USR and discovered that over time one of
the routines had become completely redundant, for a saving of 14
words. That was enough payment to get my fix put in.

If you look in V5.5 you'll see that the earlier volume check is gone,
replaced by a number of checks made to the directory header. It's in
this version that E.READ is defined (as 040000) and used. 

The problem is that if you try to use a directory which has E.READ set
with V5.4 and earlier you will get an "Invalid Directory" error
because E.READ conflicts with the earlier "^C<107400>" test.

