But there is a problem here. Can't you see it?
If you are sitting in a wait loop in the kernel, how do you get back out 
to user state in a way that will allow you to get back into the wait 
loop with a preserved context, without exposing the kernel to userspace 
manipulatio?

Normally this is not a problem, because you do not call out from kernel 
to userspace. Userspace do a system call, and eventually it returns. You 
do not have code in kernel space escaping back out into userspace, and 
then userspace returns back into kernelspace, where execution continues.

Therein lies the problem. From userspace, the system call is a single 
instruction. It is either performed, or not. Not half-done. However, by 
having the kernel call back out to user space in the middle of a system 
call, you both violate that view, and you require kernel context to be 
kept around while back in user space, at which time a context switch can 
again take place, not to mention other system calls taking place.

So, how do you keep the context for the first system call around when 
the next system call happens? And what do you expect to be on the stack? 
And what if the second system call also cause a wait? There is a big can 
of worms here.

