Don't know about RT-11, but in VMS, as well as RSX, when one AST is 
executing, all other ASTs are blocked for that process. All ASTs are 
queued for the process, and kernel ASTs are added to the head of the 
queue, while user ASTs are added to the tail.
So all ASTs (no matter which type) are delayed when an AST is in progress.

> However, there is nothing in the VMS sources about restarting system
> calls because of ASTs, or anything else for that matter.

My memory was incorrect. I can't find anything about aborting system 
calls because of ASTs. I'll look some more. The only errors meaning 
please restart system calls are from resource shortages.
However, VMS can block processes, making them deaf and dumb instead, 
which seems to be the normal behavior there. That's all your resource 
waits. No wonder VMS have lousy response times. You cannot be sure ASTs 
are delivered anywhere near the time they were queued.

And resource waits are basically mutexes for the process. And they can 
happen for a number of reasons. And no, you do not break out of a 
resource wait just because you get an AST.

In Unix, you get the system call aborted. In VMS, at some sensitive 
places you instead have the process go deaf and dumb.

Now, if you consider that better or worse than Unix, I don't know. I 
