> BTW: user mode and kernel mode have the same memory map in VMS. They
> are certainly not totally isolated. In mapped RT-11 and RUST systems
> they are quite separate.

Since in VMS (well, on a VAX anyway, but I assume on all architectures) 
you only have one memory map, that is not really relevant. User mode 
cannot access, or even see pages in the kernel. And cannot access the 
kernel stack. The kernel is totally isolated from the user mode. Kernel 
can, however, access all memory of the user. Not really different than 
from a PDP-11, even though there you have different mappings depending 
on which mode the processor is in.

The only way to get from user mode to kernel mode is by causing a trap, 
which the kernel will handle, and then do something (for example a 
system call).
You cannot "return" from user mode to kernel mode, or call random 
addresses in kernel mode from user mode, or even check any data the 
kernel holds.

