No. You are forgetting all the resource waits. These are the true 
"waiting in kernel until can continue" which is different than RSX.
The hibernate and wait-for conditions exist just like in RSX. These are 
system calls that restarted whenever interrupted. They have just about 
no context, and thus can very easily be restarted automatically, which 
is what is done.

The resource waits are something else. These are the ones where the 
system really do a wait for the process, while in the kernel (as opposed 
to the wait-for state, which essentially does not sit inside the 
kernel). And for the resource waits in VMS, the system totally blocks 
all ASTs. This is the kind of waits that I'm objecting to, and which are 
similar to the USR lock in RT-11.

Like I said - the wait-for state, as well as the hibernating, are just 
like in RSX. Although it's not called hibernate in RSX. It's probably 
most equal to the BLOCKED state, although it could also be the STOPPED 
state. (Why RSX have both is fun. They have different effects for the 
purpose of swapping, but are otherwise equal, where STOPPED means the 
swapping priority drops to 0, so that it is very eligible to be swapped 
out, while BLOCKED retain the normal swap priority. There are system 
calls to do both.)

> In VMS, the wait does take place in the mode that the system service
