Yes, VMS works like RSX. :-)
Not sure what you mean by the REI comment. Maybe the code looks 
different in VMS than RSX here, but in RSX, an AST is delivered by the 
kernel pushing the AST dependent parameters along with some context on 
the user stack, and then exit the kernel (using the REI as usual for any 
kernel operation) to get back to user state. The new PC, however, is 
then the AST address. At that point, ASTs are blocked by a piece of 
information in the process structure saying that an AST is in progress.
When your user program have finished the AST, it will do a new system 
call, the EXIT AST (ASTX$ in RSX) system call. This system call will 
pick up the context on the user stack, and will then resume by a REI 
back to user mode to continue where it was before the AST. The (old) PC 
is one of the things that were sitting on the user stack, being pushed 
there at AST entry, and popped by the AST EXIT. As a part of the AST 
EXIT, ASTs are also enabled for the process again, and before the 
process is resumed, a check is made if any ASTs have been queued. If 
they have, the first one will be processed instead of the process 
resuming at the normal context.

There are also system calls to enable and disable AST recognition. If it 
is disabled, ASTs are queued up. (ENAR$ and DSAR$ in RSX.) This is 
essentially the same state that you are in if you are currently 
processing an AST.

> So, I was right when I remembered that system calls are not restarted
