Threads really don't buy you anything if you have a proper solution 
already. Threads are a different way of solving that processes might 
hang in system calls. Do it in separate threads instead. That way, it 
becomes "ok" for things to hang in system calls. :-)

I've been thinking about writing a thread library for RSX, just for the 
fun of it. Because of the way RSX works, you can do a full thread 
implementation purely in user space.

Threads are useful in that it is easier to write programs in high level 
languages using threads than asynchronous I/O. The problem is that they 
need more memory, which is a problem on a PDP-11, and the reason I have 
not really started writing anything.

(There is also the potential problem with some HLL that they might be 
using a fixed event flag, making waits in several threads tricky to work 
in practice.)

