> Does that bear any relation to the memory in a VAXstation 3100 ?

VAXstation 3100 (Model 30, 38, 40, 48) uses memory boards that plug
into the main logic board (which had 4MB onboard).  Some memory boards
(I think 8 and 16MB) came with two connectors, others (4 and 12MB)
only one.  You could daisy-chain two boards by plugging a single
connector board into a double connector board, then plug the assembly
into the main logic board.  Max memory 32MB using a 16MB and a 12MB
board.

4MB    - MS42-AB  1993 price: $500
8MB    - MS42-KA  1993 price: $1000
12MB  - MS42-BA  1993 price: $1500
16MB  - MS42-CA  1993 price: $2000

VS3100-m76 used unique 4MB SIMMs, no embedded RAM, max 32MB.  P/N
MS44A, 1993 price $500 each

MV3100-30, 40, and 80 used pairs of SIMMs, either 4MB or 16MB each.
No embedded RAM.  32MB max (model 80 72MB max).  4MB (pair 8MB) -
MS44L-BA, 1993 cost $1000, 16MB (pair 32MB) - MS44-DA, 1993 cost $4000

MicroVAX 3100 Model 10, 10e, 20, and 20e used the same memory boards.

Old DECDirect catalogs are fun!

I'll look in the stash but I don't expect any spares survived the last
couple of moves; the boss was pretty draconian about what I could keep
when customers are no longer using the systems.  We have a 3100-30
still in service but until it is retired (any month now for 2 years ;)
I can't raid it.

If I find some I'll post here.



