 >glen herrmannsfeldt wrote:

>I believe that throughout history there have been a number
>of cases where emulators had to emulate bugs in the original
>machine. In some, the documentation was wrong and users
>expected software that depended on those bugs to work.
>  
>
Not just emulators!!  I seem to remember that FORTRAN  IV had
some bugs which were retained in later versions because many smart
users figured out ways to code around them.  Whatever the software,
the bugs were retained so the later versions would not be incompatible.

>>Second, I don't think that emulator authors are as strict as you
>>imply. For example, consider the following "programming difference"
>>from one of the J11 architecture documents:
>>
>> 30. If a register contains an odd value in mode 2 and a   [11/73]
>>     bus error occurs, the register will be incremented
>>
>>     If a register contains an odd value in mode 2 and a   [11/34]
>>     bus error occurs, the register will be unchanged.
>>
>>SimH gets it right for its default J11 CPU, where it increments 
>>and wrong for an 11/34, where it should be unchanged. E11 is the other
>>way around: right for its default 11/34 CPU, where it's unchanged and
>>wrong for the J11 where it should increment. Now these are examples of
>>incorrect implementation differences (that I would never rely on for a
>>detection test), however, there's no screaming need for SimH or E11 to
>>fix them.
>>
>There might be if there were system test programs that tested
>for those cases. When processors were less reliable, the need
>to test that one performed to specifications was important.
>  
>
I think that what Ian is saying is that when the results of the emulator 
(both SimH and
E11) differ from the actual hardware under circumstances when an error 
condition
occurs (bus error in this case) and all normal programs just quit since 
they do not
care if the register is correct or not, it is likely that both SimH and 
E11 will focus
on things that really matter.  So it is both unnecessary and unlikely 
they will be changed
to conform with the real hardware since it will never make a difference 
in any case.

That seems to me to be an excellent approach - fix the bugs that matter 
and make
enhancements that are useful.

>>The presence of a gap doesn't mean that an emulation has failed. In
>>any case SimH and E11 are quite amazing, with or without gaps.
>>
I second the motion.  I find that E11 in particular handles some situations
so well that I can't quite believe how amazing it is.  It is possible to 
move
a SCSI hard drive from a SCSI cable in a CQD 220/TM on a real DEC
system (I use a PDP-11/83) to my Pentium III running Windows 98SE
with an Adaptac 2940AU host adapter and attach the hard drive to the
50 pin SCSI cable and run the PDP-11 software directly from the SCSI
hard drive, under E11 of course, including booting the hard drive.

I understand that it is also possible with Windows XP as well, but I can't
confirm that as yet.

Jerome Fine


