The logic is not just combinational; there is plenty of register state and =
internal buses in a CPU. An undefined opcode could definitely trigger micro=
- or nano-code (aside, I know nothing about PDP-11 internals) that does "ba=
d" things like drive two registers simultaneously onto an internal bus, or =
latches a value from a bus that is floating. The result of these is "determ=
inistic" in the sense of physics, but not in terms of digital logic. A floa=
ting bus might or might not have a valid logic value; that depends on the i=
nternal history (the last valid value driven, and what exact voltage that d=
river used) analog conditions (capacitance & leakage) and timing that are o=
utside the digital definitions. Two outputs fighting one another might have=
 a deterministic outcome, or a fight between driving low & driving high mig=
ht leave the line in the range of voltages that aren't defined as either lo=
w or high, and anything reading from this voltage has no guarantees about w=
hat it does.



