<snip>

>>> We might read things in a different way. "Undefined" is, in this
>>> circumstance, as far as I can tell, only something that is valid as far
>>> as a general view of a PDP-11 is concerned. If you emulate a specific
>>> implementation, the behavior is not undefined. And Simh aims to
>>> implement the same twists as the machine it pretends to emulate. You
>>> have found a case where it does not behave the same way as the machine
>>> it claims to emulate. I'd say that this means Simh have a bug.
>>>
>>> Undefined generally only means that different implementations of a
>>> PDP-11 are free to do this as they see fit, and no program should depend
>>> on a specific behavior.
>>> However, a specific implementation will only do it one way or another.
>>
>> I have been using "undefined" in the architectural sense, that is,
>> behavior which is not specified by the *architecture* or explicitly
>> specified as "undefined". That's fairly well-known terminology.
>
>Excellent. So we are using it the same way.

No, we ain't!

>So when E11 is configured to simulate a J11, it is expected to do things 
>the same way as a J11, and if you managed to find corner cases where it 
>doesn't, then it is a bug in E11, since it is expected to behave the 
>same as a J11. And the same for Simh. That is why all those options in 
>the config exist - to make the emulator implement all the odd behavior 
>of that specific implementation.

I'm talking about the architecture:

By "architecture" I mean the specification of the system in the
relevant documents. For example, the 11/73 is specified by the
documents in http://www.bitsavers.org/pdf/dec/pdp11/1173/. The
relevant XXDP processor tests provide further clarification of the
architecture. In some cases it may be necessary to refer to the actual
hardware if the specification is fuzzy.

You're talking about the implementation:

By "implementation" I mean the behavior of the actual machine. It may
have behaviors which are not part of the architecture or which are
specified by the architecture "undefined"  or "unpredictable".
The implementation behavior may contain "don't care" states which are
not specified or defined by the architecture. 

>E11 (or Simh) is not a generic PDP-11 implementation, but aims to 
>emulate specific models, with all their quirks.

That doesn't have to be an issue as we can consider each emulated
model separately.

>> Clearly, there can be *no* undefined *implementation* behavior and
>> thus the distinction between "defined" and "undefined" is meaningless
>> if applied to the implementation.
>
>Right.
>
>> But to take your point that an emulation could avoid "undefined"
>> architecture states by referring to "unspecified implementation
>> behavior" I'd make two arguments.
>
>I'm not sure I made that point. I think my point was that the emulator 
>don't have any "undefined" behavior. It claims to emulate a specific 
>system/model, and thus it should (obviously) have the same quirks as the 
>system it emulates.
>Your response that you are exploiting "undefined" behavior to find out 
>what system it is, is, in my view, invalid for an emulator that aims to 
>emulate another system. It should not be possible to tell the emulation 
>apart from the system it emulates. Otherwise the emulation is buggy. So, 
>any trick you figured out to detect that you are running under Simh is, 
>by definition, a bug in Simh.

The point depends on the distinction between "architecture" and
"implementation" made above...

>> Second, I don't think that emulator authors are as strict as you
>> imply.
>
>I think that Bob definitely are that strict. And I'm pretty sure that 
>John in general are too, since he have gone to great lengths to 
>implement the specific behavior of different implementations of the 
>PDP-11 for these undefined things.

But I don't think you're consistent in your expectations. See below.

>> For example, consider the following "programming difference"
>> from one of the J11 architecture documents:
>>
>>    30. If a register contains an odd value in mode 2 and a   [11/73]
>>        bus error occurs, the register will be incremented
>>
>>        If a register contains an odd value in mode 2 and a   [11/34]
>>        bus error occurs, the register will be unchanged.
>>
>> SimH gets it right for its default J11 CPU, where it increments
>> and wrong for an 11/34, where it should be unchanged. E11 is the other
>> way around: right for its default 11/34 CPU, where it's unchanged and
>> wrong for the J11 where it should increment. Now these are examples of
>> incorrect implementation differences (that I would never rely on for a
>> detection test), however, there's no screaming need for SimH or E11 to
