I got a better idea just after I pushed SEND (by mistake).

The idea is to do the "terminal delay" test twice, counting the number
of instructions for each of four output characters. 

Loop 1: The first time through the instruction counting loop looks
like this:

10$:	inc	(r0)		; count instructions
	tstb	(r1)		; r1 -> TPS
	bne	10$		; more
				; 5 memory references

Loop 2: The second loop looks like this:

20$:	add	@one,@cnt	; inc (r0)
	bitb	@rdy,@sts	; tstb tps
	bne	20$		; more
				; 11 memory references
one:	.word	.+2,1
cnt:	etc

The difference in the number of memory references should affect the
counts for a real PDP-11 but have no affect on E11 or SimH.

The first loop has five memory references per iteration. The second
has eleven. On a real PDP-11 more than twice as many instructions will
occur in the first loop than in the second. Under E11 or SimH the loop
counts will be constant.

Loop 1           Loop2
157 155 156 155  56  57  56  55  Real PDP-1
42  42  42  42   42  42  42  42  SimH or E11

This test is reasonably short and fairly definitive in testing the
difference between a standard "real" PDP-11 system and an emulated
system running under SimH or E11.

The test doesn't discriminate between E11 and SimH; that's a separate
task.

Here's the rough test program I put together and which I ran on an
11/23, E11 and SimH. The second line of the source is modified for
non-RUST systems. You have to examine the table c$utab using the
console debugger to get the results.

.title	ttnulx - detect emulator via TT nulls
;.include "lib:rust.mac"   ; RUST
.mcall	.mtps              ; RT-11

;	%build
;	macro ets:ttnulx/object:etb:
;	link etb:ttnulx/exe:etb:
;	%end

	tps = 177564
	tpb = 177566

c$utab:	.word	0, 0, 0, 0
	.word	0, 0, 0, 0

start:	.mtps	#340
10$:	mov	#c$utab,r0
	mov	#4,r1
	mov	#tps,r2
	call	synch
20$:	clr	(r0)
	clrb	@#tpb
30$:	inc	(r0)
	tstb	(r2)
	bpl	30$
	tst	(r0)+
	dec	r1
	bne	20$

	call	synch
	mov	#4,r1
40$:	clr	(r0)
	mov	r0,c$upnt
	clrb	@#tpb
50$:	add	@c$uone,@c$upnt
	bitb	@c$u200,@c$utps
	beq	50$
	tst	(r0)+
	dec	r1
	bne	40$

	halt
	mov	#c$utab,r0
	br	10$

c$uone:	.word	.+2,1
c$u200:	.word	.+2,200
c$upnt:	.word	0
c$utps:	.word	tps

synch:
10$:	tstb	@#tps
	bpl	10$
	clr	@#tpb
20$:	tstb	@#tps
	bpl	20$
	return

	.end	start

