>> We in fact must. As Simh does not implement any PDP-11 based on the
>> architecture, but instead just tries to simulate a number of existing
>> implementations.
>
> As there isn't, as far as I know, a reference architecture
> for the PDP-11. Even in system that do have a defined architecture,
> they may change it. There are 11 different versions of the IBM
> S/370 Principles of Operation, not to mention the many optional
> features. Also, implementations of S/370 have optional features,
> and so the use of those could be undefined.

I believe that the processor handbooks are the architecture reference 
for the PDP-11.

> As I previously mentioned, S/370 has a "diagnose" instruction,
> specifically defined to have undefined properties, including the
> reporting of faults in the machine. Some might be documented for
> the specific implementation, but not all.

Some PDP-11 models also have diagnose instructions that are very 
specific to the model. It is very undefined for the architecture, but 
for a specific model, it is very defined.

>>> The point depends on the distinction between "architecture" and
>>> "implementation" made above...
>
>> I think you are using the term "architecture" wrong, if you think
>> that a PDP-11/73 is an architecture.
>
> Probably. DEC defined an architecture for VAX, I have the book
> that says so. If they didn't for the PDP-11, then it isn't so
> obvious.

For the VAX, DEC produced the very obviously named VARM (VAX 
Architecture Reference Manual), which outlines the architecture. For the 
PDP-11 I know of no such named manual with the same status. I believe 
that the processor handbooks in general fulfill that role. I don't know 
of any of them giving contradictory information, but later ones have 
extensions that didn't exist when the earlier were printed.

Some of the last ones also have tables describing the differences in 
actual implementations, which goes beyond the architecture, which just 
says it's undefined.

