In vmsnet.pdp-11 Johnny Billquist <bqt@softjar.se> wrote:

(snip, someone wrote)
>> But isn't the inability to tell you are running on an emulator the
>> true proof that the emulator works?

> Right. But Jerome, for some reason, wants to do just this, if I 
> understand him correctly.

There is a tradition of emulators (or virtual machines) at least 
back to VM/370, of allowing the guest to do things no real machine
allows. That is, talk to the emulator. To do that, there should
be a way to tell if that feature is available.

Now, one can always put it in the configuration file for the guest.
As I previously mentioned, there were DOS device drivers for the
OS/2 emulation of DOS that allowed for this. 

There is a feature for OS/VS1 running under VM/370 to avoid
delays due to paging. As both OS/VS1 and VM/370 are virtual
memory system, paging when needed, it is possible for OS/VS1
to be waiting for a page from VM when it has other tasks that
could run. Also, it is most efficient to let VM/370 do all the
paging. Using the special feature, VM/370 can notify OS/VS1 that
it should run another task while VM pages in the first one.

All in all, there are many cases where having the emulator
allow things that real hardware never did can be useful.

-- glen


