>>>have the explicitly stated goal that it should not work. Any current 
>>>differences from the real machine are bugs, and are subject to be fixed 
>>>at any time, at which point the test wont work anymore.
>>
>> That's not exactly what was stated. Without wishing to put words into
>> anyone's mouth, BobS wrote this:
>>
>>   The model emulation in SimH is not perfect, which is why clever
>>   software can detect it. But the intent is to mimic real systems.
>>   SimH is not a "different" PDP11.
>>
>> And what I wrote earlier was this:
>>
>>   The trick was to find some "undefined" behavior that didn't need
>>   correction. The code above gets different results from an F11 and a
>>   J11 as well as from SimH and E11. The test is far from ideal, but it
>>   works well enough for my purposes.
>>
>> My point being, that I'm not sure that the behavior I'm using needs to
>> be "corrected" in the sense that I'm not sure it's actually "defined".
>> I'm not even sure that the behavior I'm using can be corrected without
>> a considerable effort. (One reason I'm not sure is that I wrote the
>> code years ago and I've forgotten whether the result values could be
>> computed or would need to be (at least partially) enumerated). 
>
>Seems like a distinction without a difference. Changing behavior with
>a purpose (correcting) and changing undefined behavior with or without
>a purpose seems to have the same result. I would agree the risk of a
>relatively mature simulator changing significantly seems acceptable,
>but relying on undefined behavior is still building your house on
>sand.
>
>In the back of my mind I have warning bells going off screaming "how
>hard will this be to debug if the undefined behavior changes?"

You raise an interesting distinction between real PDP-11s and emulated
PDP-11s. The behavior, defined or undefined, of a real PDP-11 does not
change. Thus, one might also claim that a goal of a emulator should be
also be to remain constant.

To follow your point to its logical conclusion would mean that I would
have to simply give up on trying to detect an emulator in its standard
deliverable form. I don't want to do that. 

Clearly, building my house on an error in defined behavior would be
utterly ridiculous because, being a good citizen, I would
simultaneously advise the author of an emulator of the error, which
would usually lead to an alteration. Thus "risk" doesn't really apply
here as failure is a near certainty.

In the case of undefined behavior the situation changes: there is no
compelling reason for undefined behavior to be altered unless such a
change leads to a material improvement in the emulation, particularly
when the emulator is mature.

As to your screaming bells: no critical functionality currently relies
on the outcome of the test. The only decision made on the basis of the
test is whether or whether not to proceed with a further timing loop
test, and that test also fails benignly. I would need to add further
detection and sanity tests, or to be satisfied that the emulator would
not change, before I would rely on the functionality significantly. 

I am currently considering basing one bit of functionality on the E11
and SimH tests and that's to use a WAIT instruction to reduce CPU
hogging. But I would consider that change to be non-critical (after
the appropriate testing).

By way of comparison, I also do a detection test for the V11 emulator
using the V11 emulator API (based on an instruction, not a device :-).
That test is definitive and does lead to altered behavior of the
system.

Thus, the current E11/SimH detection functionality is largely
exploratory and envisaged as long-term settling-in process. One value
of the tests is to raise the issue that we are discussing in this
thread: should emulators be detectable or not. Clearly, I think that
they should be, but I accept that those who see emulators entirely as
historical vehicles would have another opinion.

Ian


