 >Johnny Billquist wrote:

> [Snip]
> E11 does not do it synchronously. It's done in different threads, that 
> can complete asynchronously to each other. Which is nice. It means 
> that your CPU does not stand still just because you issued an 
> operation to a device. Especially if you have a multi-cpu host machine.

My discussions with John Wilson suggest that the different
versions of E11 are different for I/O.

At the moment, the DOS version is still synchronous (as
far as I remember).

The Win32 version may still be synchronous, but there
is definitely the intention to all for non-synchronous
disk I/O since there is more than one thread and the
disk I/O can be done in one thread while the CPU is
able to proceed (being executed) in the other thread.

Please do NOT rely on this information!!  It is meant
FYI (for your information) and may be incorrect.

I just wanted to correct the suggestion by Johnny that
E11 definitely supports non-sychronous disk I/O
like both RT-11 and RSX-11 does on native DEC
CPUs.

Jerome Fine

