In vmsnet.pdp-11 Johnny Billquist <bqt@softjar.se> wrote:

(snip)
> Ah, sorry. Yes, I should probably point out that all my experience is 
> with E11 under Linux...
> Other versions of E11 might be synchronous. However, for the topic, that 
> is not really relevant. The important thing is/was that there are 
> emulators that will not do I/O operation synchronously, so that can not 
> be used as a criteria to detect them in general.

Well, even if not synchronous, an I/O operations might still
complete faster than on a real processor with an appropriate I/O
device. 

It seems, though, that there are multiple uses for the word
synchronous when describing I/O. 

In the I/O system used by IBM for S/360, S/370, and even later,
there was pretty much no buffering between the I/O device and
storage. As with floppy disk controllers now, one byte would be
assembled from the serial bit stream and written to memory.
Later, that might have increased to 16 bits or 32 bits, 
but not much more. That is what IBM calls synchronous I/O.
Among others, this allowed for self-modifying channel programs
where recently read I/O commands were then executed.

Later on, control units with block or track sized buffers allowed
for 'asynchronous I/O', such that the contents of memory did
not change while bits were coming off disk.

For SCSI, it is related to the timing on the SCSI bus,
with synchronous timing allowing for faster transfers.
(Asynchronous SCSI requries a two-way handshake for each byte.)

The low-end S/360 and S/370 models used the same logic,
through microprogramming, for both CPU and I/O operations,
such that I/O was pretty much, in the sense here, synchronous.

-- glen


