On 2012-02-02 19:52, glen herrmannsfeldt wrote:
> In vmsnet.pdp-11 Johnny Billquist<bqt@softjar.se>  wrote:
>
> (snip)
>> Ah, sorry. Yes, I should probably point out that all my experience is
>> with E11 under Linux...
>> Other versions of E11 might be synchronous. However, for the topic, that
>> is not really relevant. The important thing is/was that there are
>> emulators that will not do I/O operation synchronously, so that can not
>> be used as a criteria to detect them in general.
>
> Well, even if not synchronous, an I/O operations might still
> complete faster than on a real processor with an appropriate I/O
> device.

Certainly. And the emulator might manage to do way more instructions in 
that shorter time than a real machine with a slower device.
Not only I/O have gotten faster. CPUs have too...

> It seems, though, that there are multiple uses for the word
> synchronous when describing I/O.

Maybe. I'm using it in the meaning that from the PDP-11 point of view, 
the completion of an I/O request does not happen in a predetermined 
(often instant) amount of time. It is asynchronous to the execution of 
instructions in the emulator. I/O completes at a later, undefined time.

Did that specify it enough, or did I manage to fuddle it up?

